      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "After decades of annexation attempts, residents voted against Atlanta in 1966, sparking a movement that led to Sandy Springs' incorporation in 2005.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        13,
        28,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sandy_Springs%2C_Georgia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The 1960s construction of Georgia 400 and Interstate 285 linked Sandy Springs to metro Atlanta, igniting a housing boom during the post‑Civil Rights era.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sandy_Springs%2C_Georgia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Facing no downtown, city leaders built City Springs, a mixed‑use hub with offices, homes, retail, green space, and a performing‑arts center.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sandy_Springs%2C_Georgia",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Heritage Sandy Springs Museum, opened in 2010, preserves the town’s early history and showcases artifacts from its Native American and pioneer eras.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sandy_Springs%2C_Georgia",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
